NAME
    memfind - locate a byte sequence in a memory space

SYNOPSIS
    memfind <space> <bytes> [<max>]

DESCRIPTION
    Scans memory space <space> in one pass for every offset matching the
    hex byte pattern <bytes>. Unlike search(1), which narrows a 1/2/4-byte
    scalar across frames, 'memfind' matches arbitrary multi-byte blocks
    such as tile data, strings, or decompressed buffers.

    <bytes> consists of hex byte pairs; ',', ':', '-', '_', and whitespace
    between pairs are ignored (so '00FF11', '00 FF 11', and '00:FF:11' are
    equivalent). Use '??' as a wildcard matching any single byte.

    Stops after <max> hits (default 20, decimal > 0) and notes when more
    matches exist. Refuses spaces backed by live hardware registers with
    read side effects (such as CpuBus).

EXAMPLES
    memfind WRAM 00FFFFFFFF00FF00
    memfind VRAM 00??FF??11 5
    memfind ROM 4E696E74656E646F

EXIT STATUS
    0   Scan completed (or usage/refusal message printed).
    1   No target attached, unknown space, or session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    search(1), mem(1), spaces(1), dump(1)
